WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.275
Leverage of 10 lets you open a 1,000-dollar position with 100 of collateral.

2
00:00:05.275 --> 00:00:09.033
A one percent price move is ten percent of the collateral.

3
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:12.014
When the loss has almost eaten the collateral,

4
00:00:12.014 --> 00:00:14.606
the exchange closes the position itself.

5
00:00:14.606 --> 00:00:16.680
The main trap is the price path:

6
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.503
after a 12 percent fall the position is already liquidated,

7
00:00:20.503 --> 00:00:23.030
and a 20 percent rise will not help it.

8
00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:28.984
With isolated margin, liquidation takes only the collateral of that position.

9
00:00:28.984 --> 00:00:32.265
With cross margin, the whole account is collateral:

10
00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:37.090
liquidation is pushed further away, but a bad position can take everything.

11
00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:42.559
And liquidation is calculated on the mark price, an average across several exchanges,

12
00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:46.998
so that one random spike does not close everyone's positions at once.

13
00:00:47.998 --> 00:00:49.001
The main points.

14
00:00:49.001 --> 00:00:50.694
The price is an order book:

15
00:00:50.694 --> 00:00:53.453
a market order pays the spread and slippage.

16
00:00:53.453 --> 00:00:55.397
The fee for a round seems tiny,

17
00:00:55.397 --> 00:01:00.162
but with frequent trading it eats capital faster than any forecasting error.

18
00:01:00.162 --> 00:01:04.739
And leverage of 10 liquidates on a move of about ten percent against you,

19
00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:06.934
even if the price later comes back.
